Real code:

// From the PARKING LOT project (class-29)
public class SpotFactory {

    private static final Map<SpotSize, ISpot> strategies = new HashMap<>();

    static {
        strategies.put(SpotSize.COMPACT, new CompactSpot());
        strategies.put(SpotSize.LARGE,   new LargeSpot());
        strategies.put(SpotSize.HEAVY,   new HeavySpot());
    }

    public ISpot getSpot(SpotSize spotSize) {
        return strategies.get(spotSize);
    }
}

This is the parking lot's spot factory from class-29. Its map is filled once, in a static block, when the class first loads. If a caller does:

    ISpot a = spotFactory.getSpot(SpotSize.COMPACT);
    ISpot b = spotFactory.getSpot(SpotSize.COMPACT);

what is the relationship between a and b?,a and b are two separate CompactSpot objects with identical values,"a and b are the exact same object - getSpot() returns the one CompactSpot that was created in the static block, every single time","b is a copy of a, so changing one does not affect the other","b is null, because the entry was consumed by the first call",,2,"Verified by running it: (a == b) prints true. The map is a fixed lookup table of three pre-built objects, so the method hands out references to the same three objects forever.",2

