// Main.java of the parking lot project
groundFloor.addSpot(spotFactory.getSpot(SpotSize.COMPACT));
groundFloor.addSpot(spotFactory.getSpot(SpotSize.COMPACT));
groundFloor.addSpot(spotFactory.getSpot(SpotSize.LARGE));
groundFloor.addSpot(spotFactory.getSpot(SpotSize.HEAVY));

The floor now holds 4 entries and reports availableSpots() == 4. One car parks in a compact spot. Running this, availableSpots() then reports 2, not 3. Why?,The car occupied two spots because it was too large for one,"Both compact entries in the list are the same object, so marking it occupied makes both entries report occupied at once",availableSpots() has an off-by-one error in its loop,The LARGE spot was also reserved for the car,,2,"Measured output: 'two COMPACT calls same object? true', then availableSpots() drops 4 -> 2 after a single assign(). The building believes it lost two parking spaces because it only ever had one compact object pretending to be two.",2

