public class PaymentStrategyFactory {

    public IPaymentStrategy getStrategy(PaymentMethod method) {
        if (method == PaymentMethod.CASH) {
            return new CashPayment();
        } else if (method == PaymentMethod.CARD) {
            return new CardPayment();
        }
        else if (method == PaymentMethod.SPECIAL) {
            return new SpecialCardPayment();
        }
        else {
            return new UPIPayment();
        }
    }
}

The spot factory caches one instance per kind. The payment factory builds a new object on every call. Both are 'factories'. What actually decides which approach is correct?,Caching is always faster and therefore always preferable,"Whether the produced objects carry state. A parking spot remembers whether it is occupied, so sharing one is a bug. The payment classes hold only final constants and remember nothing between calls, so sharing them would have been harmless",Whether the factory method is static or not,Whether the objects implement an interface or extend an abstract class,,2,"This is the transferable rule from the two projects side by side: cache stateless objects freely, never cache stateful ones. The vending machine got away with 'new' every time because there is nothing to get wrong.",2

Real code:

public class PaymentStrategyFactory {

    public IPaymentStrategy getStrategy(PaymentMethod method) {
        if (method == PaymentMethod.CASH) {
            return new CashPayment();
        } else if (method == PaymentMethod.CARD) {
            return new CardPayment();
        }
        else if (method == PaymentMethod.SPECIAL) {
            return new SpecialCardPayment();
        }
        else {
            return new UPIPayment();
        }
    }
}

Look carefully at the final else. A new value, WALLET, is added to the PaymentMethod enum, and a developer forgets to add a matching branch here. What happens when a customer pays by wallet?,"A compile error, so the mistake is caught immediately","It silently falls into the final else and is processed as a UPI payment, quietly deducting UPI's 0.10 fee - with no error anywhere",getStrategy returns null and the facade throws NullPointerException,An IllegalArgumentException is thrown naming the unhandled method,,2,"A catch-all else turns 'I forgot a case' into 'I chose UPI'. Nothing in the code, and nothing in the output, indicates the wallet branch is missing.",2

Real code:

public class PaymentStrategyFactory {

    public IPaymentStrategy getStrategy(PaymentMethod method) {
        if (method == PaymentMethod.CASH) {
            return new CashPayment();
        } else if (method == PaymentMethod.CARD) {
            return new CardPayment();
        }
        else if (method == PaymentMethod.SPECIAL) {
            return new SpecialCardPayment();
        }
        else {
            return new UPIPayment();
        }
    }
}

Following on from the missing-branch problem: which change would make that mistake impossible to miss?,Rename the else branch to something clearer,"Give UPI its own explicit branch and make the final else throw, e.g. IllegalArgumentException(""unsupported payment method: "" + method) - so an unmapped enum value fails loudly instead of impersonating UPI",Move the factory into the same package as PaymentMethod,Make getStrategy static,,2,Same principle the state classes already follow: an unhandled case must announce itself. A default branch that quietly returns a real working object is the worst of both worlds.,1

